{"connection_id": "BC-00d54f945336b76ce75a", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.25", "target": "sec15", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "imperishable crown", "note": "The commentary calls the later life more enduring and compares it to a horse saving its run; Paul contrasts athletes' perishable wreath with the imperishable one sought by Christians, joining race discipline to lasting reward."}
{"connection_id": "BC-2e0eaff067bfb6e6a41c", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.25", "target": "sec15", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "perishable and imperishable wreath", "note": "Paul joins an athlete's self-control to a perishable versus imperishable crown, closely pairing the commentary's run held in reserve with its contrast between what passes and what endures."}
{"connection_id": "BC-a69d79e4bb082abe2438", "ref": "SBLGNT:1Cor.9.27", "target": "sec15", "tradition": "incil", "kind": "motif", "basis": "discipline or risk disqualification", "note": "The commentary says an early position does not guarantee the final win; Paul disciplines his body lest, after preaching to others, he himself be disqualified, adding the possibility that even a runner can fail at the end."}
